DISCLOSURE 2. TWO SPEC UNDERSPECIFICATIONS WERE RESOLVED BY AUTHORITY RULING INSIDE THE PLAN BEFORE APPROVAL, on points the executor flagged rather than routed around. JSON keys are moduleId and adapterId, not module and adapter, because LanguageAnalysis already names that value adapterId and one value must not acquire a second name. A resolved module with no Path stays not-found, since the module was found and the Path was not, provided the two messages name different causes - which F72 then showed the code was not doing.

DISCLOSURE 3. A THIRD DECISION WAS REVERSED BY THE AUTHORITY BEFORE SPEC APPROVAL. The draft had unverifiedLanguages not suppressing graph.path-no-adapter. It now suppresses. The existing warning's own text instructs the author to acknowledge with that key, and the new code names the same consequence for the same extension, so a second code firing after the documented acknowledgement would make the acknowledgement false - F10 in the product rather than mere noise. The fact survives suppression because ngrace status still reports the unverified count, as the executor's Case C measured. The trigger Constraint, the AC skip list and its failure conditions were edited by the authority before approval.

